course: dictionary
lesson: 1267
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word bush can mean a plant, wild land far from towns, or a thick mass of hair.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «He raced off into the bushes like the wind», the bushes are places to hide among, so they are plants. In «Rose bushes» in a garden, the word tells you it is a plant. In «bushwalking» in Australia, it means wild land.
explain: To check, put each meaning into the sentence and see which one fits the clues best. The meaning that makes sense every time is the right one.
fact: A bush is a plant like a small tree with many branches close to the ground.
fact: In Australia, New Zealand and Africa, «the bush» means wild land far from towns, with trees and shrubs.
fact: A bush of hair is a thick mass of hair.
fact: «Beat around the bush» means not saying something directly.
fact: A «bushwalk» is a walk through the bush, which means wild land.
exercise: «Animals hid in the bushes at the edge of the garden.» What does bushes mean here? | A small wooden shed | Wild land far from towns | A thick mass of hair | *Plants with many branches close to the ground
exercise: «Her hair was a bush of curls after the wind.» What does bush mean here? | Wild land far from towns | A plant in the garden | A type of bird | *A thick mass of hair
exercise: «We went bushwalking in the bush outside Sydney.» What does the bush mean in this sentence? | *Wild land far from towns, with trees and shrubs | A plant with prickles | A thick mass of hair | A small tree in a pot
test: «He planted rose bushes in a neat row by the fence.» What does bushes mean here? | A thick mass of hair | *Plants with many branches close to the ground | Wild land far from towns | Berries picked for jam
test: Dad says, «Stop beating around the bush and tell me the truth!» What does this phrase mean? | *Stop avoiding the point and say it directly | Go and look for a bird | Cut down all the plants | Walk through the wild land quickly
exam: «The bush was on fire, and the smoke spread for miles over the hills.» What does bush most likely mean here? | A hedge in a school garden | *Wild land with trees and shrubs far from towns | A small plant in a pot | A thick mass of hair
